1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:02,886
You've already used AI today.

2
00:00:03,206 --> 00:00:04,764
Probably before breakfast.

3
00:00:05,084 --> 00:00:07,534
Your phone recognised your face and
unlocked.

4
00:00:07,854 --> 00:00:10,863
Your email quietly moved a scam into the
spam folder.

5
00:00:11,183 --> 00:00:13,747
Your map guessed the traffic and changed
your route.

6
00:00:14,067 --> 00:00:16,263
Your keyboard finished a word before you
did.

7
00:00:16,583 --> 00:00:19,594
None of it said "artificial intelligence"
on the box.

8
00:00:19,914 --> 00:00:21,850
It just worked, somewhere you couldn't
see.

9
00:00:22,992 --> 00:00:25,461
Then, a few years ago, a new kind arrived.

10
00:00:25,781 --> 00:00:27,032
The kind you can talk to.

11
00:00:27,352 --> 00:00:30,273
It writes, explains, translates and argues
back.

12
00:00:30,593 --> 00:00:33,567
And almost all of it runs on someone
else's computer.

13
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:38,646
This course is about understanding that
kind of AI, from zero.

14
00:00:38,966 --> 00:00:42,356
And by the last lesson, you'll have one
running on your own computer.

15
00:00:42,676 --> 00:00:43,562
No account.

16
00:00:43,882 --> 00:00:44,950
No subscription.

17
00:00:45,270 --> 00:00:46,496
No internet needed.

18
00:00:54,658 --> 00:00:56,737
Welcome to AI101.

19
00:00:57,057 --> 00:00:59,622
It's for people who have never trained a
model,

20
00:00:59,622 --> 00:01:03,387
never opened a terminal, and maybe aren't
sure what a model even is.

21
00:01:03,707 --> 00:01:05,755
That's exactly the right place to start.

22
00:01:06,908 --> 00:01:07,788
Here's the deal.

23
00:01:08,108 --> 00:01:08,813
It's free.

24
00:01:09,133 --> 00:01:12,540
There are no sponsors, no affiliate links,
and nothing to buy.

25
00:01:12,860 --> 00:01:17,346
When we use a tool, we'll tell you why, and we'll show you at least one
alternative.

26
00:01:17,666 --> 00:01:21,672
We'll be honest about what this technology
can't do, not just what it can.

27
00:01:21,992 --> 00:01:23,119
And we'll go slowly.

28
00:01:23,439 --> 00:01:26,089
Every word gets explained the first time
it appears.

29
00:01:27,242 --> 00:01:28,133
What do you need?

30
00:01:28,593 --> 00:01:29,407
A computer.

31
00:01:29,727 --> 00:01:33,825
If it's from the last few years and has at
least eight gigabytes of memory,

32
00:01:33,825 --> 00:01:35,901
it can very likely run a small model.

33
00:01:36,221 --> 00:01:39,119
Lesson five will tell you exactly what
fits yours.

34
00:01:39,439 --> 00:01:40,578
You don't need to code.

35
00:01:40,898 --> 00:01:43,006
You don't need an expensive graphics card.

36
00:01:43,326 --> 00:01:44,614
You don't need to pay anyone.

37
00:01:45,783 --> 00:01:46,695
One more thing.

38
00:01:47,015 --> 00:01:49,126
Tools in this field change every month.

39
00:01:49,446 --> 00:01:51,290
So the videos explain the ideas,

40
00:01:51,290 --> 00:01:55,264
and the exact steps live in a written
guide that we keep up to date.

41
00:01:55,584 --> 00:01:57,165
The link is in the description.

42
00:02:00,908 --> 00:02:04,252
Let's start with the words, because people
use them loosely,

43
00:02:04,252 --> 00:02:06,258
and that causes a lot of confusion.

44
00:02:06,578 --> 00:02:09,150
Artificial intelligence is the biggest
circle.

45
00:02:09,470 --> 00:02:10,550
It's the whole field:

46
00:02:10,550 --> 00:02:14,713
getting machines to do things that would
need intelligence if a person did them.

47
00:02:15,033 --> 00:02:16,375
Recognising a face.

48
00:02:16,695 --> 00:02:17,661
Planning a route.

49
00:02:17,981 --> 00:02:18,874
Playing chess.

50
00:02:19,194 --> 00:02:20,640
Understanding a sentence.

51
00:02:21,783 --> 00:02:25,798
For decades, the main way to do that was
to write the rules by hand.

52
00:02:26,118 --> 00:02:28,273
Imagine building a spam filter that way.

53
00:02:28,593 --> 00:02:30,930
If the email says "free money", it's spam.

54
00:02:31,250 --> 00:02:32,904
If it says "you won", it's spam.

55
00:02:33,224 --> 00:02:37,063
It works, until the scammers write "fr3e
m0ney".

56
00:02:37,383 --> 00:02:38,720
Then you need another rule.

57
00:02:39,040 --> 00:02:39,730
And another.

58
00:02:40,867 --> 00:02:43,050
Machine learning is the next circle in.

59
00:02:43,370 --> 00:02:46,673
Instead of writing the rules, you show the
computer examples.

60
00:02:46,993 --> 00:02:50,275
Thousands of emails marked spam, thousands
marked fine.

61
00:02:50,595 --> 00:02:52,735
The program finds the patterns itself,

62
00:02:52,735 --> 00:02:56,058
including ones no person would have
thought to write down.

63
00:02:57,200 --> 00:02:59,268
Deep learning is a circle inside that.

64
00:02:59,588 --> 00:03:02,394
It's machine learning done with neural
networks:

65
00:03:02,394 --> 00:03:05,492
long chains of simple maths, stacked in
many layers.

66
00:03:05,812 --> 00:03:07,915
Each layer passes numbers to the next.

67
00:03:08,235 --> 00:03:12,142
During training, millions or billions of
those numbers are nudged,

68
00:03:12,142 --> 00:03:15,221
a tiny bit at a time, until the answers
get better.

69
00:03:15,541 --> 00:03:17,213
"Deep" just means many layers.

70
00:03:17,533 --> 00:03:19,521
It's not deep in any mystical sense.

71
00:03:19,841 --> 00:03:22,843
It needs a lot of data and a lot of
computing power,

72
00:03:22,843 --> 00:03:26,539
which is why it only really took off in
the last fifteen years.

73
00:03:27,700 --> 00:03:32,747
And inside deep learning is the circle this course is mostly about: language
models.

74
00:03:33,067 --> 00:03:35,709
A language model is a neural network
trained

75
00:03:35,709 --> 00:03:38,411
on enormous amounts of text to do one
thing:

76
00:03:38,411 --> 00:03:39,913
predict what comes next.

77
00:03:40,233 --> 00:03:42,072
That sounds too simple to be useful.

78
00:03:42,392 --> 00:03:44,196
Lesson two shows why it isn't.

79
00:03:44,516 --> 00:03:47,823
The chatbots you've heard of are language
models, wrapped in an app.

80
00:03:48,992 --> 00:03:51,189
You'll also hear "generative AI".

81
00:03:51,509 --> 00:03:55,960
That means models that create something
new: text, images, sound, video.

82
00:03:56,280 --> 00:03:58,078
Language models are one kind.

83
00:03:58,398 --> 00:04:00,072
Image generators are another.

84
00:04:01,242 --> 00:04:01,756
So.

85
00:04:02,076 --> 00:04:03,445
AI is the field.

86
00:04:03,765 --> 00:04:06,199
Machine learning is learning from
examples.

87
00:04:06,519 --> 00:04:09,777
Deep learning is doing that with
many-layered neural networks.

88
00:04:10,097 --> 00:04:13,044
And language models are deep learning
trained on text.

89
00:04:16,783 --> 00:04:18,827
None of this started in 2022.

90
00:04:19,992 --> 00:04:21,032
1950.

91
00:04:21,352 --> 00:04:26,215
The mathematician Alan Turing publishes a
paper that opens with a simple question:

92
00:04:26,215 --> 00:04:27,402
can machines think?

93
00:04:27,862 --> 00:04:30,356
Instead of answering it, he proposes a
test.

94
00:04:30,676 --> 00:04:33,586
If you can't tell a machine from a person
in conversation,

95
00:04:33,586 --> 00:04:34,991
does the difference matter?

96
00:04:36,158 --> 00:04:37,480
1956.

97
00:04:37,800 --> 00:04:39,793
A small group of researchers spends a

98
00:04:39,793 --> 00:04:42,486
summer at Dartmouth College in the United
States.

99
00:04:42,806 --> 00:04:44,638
The proposal for that workshop is

100
00:04:44,638 --> 00:04:47,579
where the name "artificial intelligence"
comes from.

101
00:04:47,899 --> 00:04:50,524
They expected real progress in a single
summer.

102
00:04:51,658 --> 00:04:52,892
1958.

103
00:04:53,212 --> 00:04:55,863
Frank Rosenblatt introduces the
perceptron,

104
00:04:55,863 --> 00:05:00,364
an early machine that learns to recognise
simple patterns from examples.

105
00:05:00,684 --> 00:05:03,148
It's a grandparent of every neural network
today.

106
00:05:04,283 --> 00:05:05,668
1966.

107
00:05:05,988 --> 00:05:08,855
Joseph Weizenbaum at MIT writes ELIZA,

108
00:05:08,855 --> 00:05:15,117
a program that imitates a therapist by turning your sentences back into
questions.

109
00:05:15,437 --> 00:05:16,682
It understands nothing.

110
00:05:17,002 --> 00:05:18,960
And still, people confided in it.

111
00:05:20,117 --> 00:05:24,139
Weizenbaum later wrote that his own
secretary, who had watched him build it,

112
00:05:24,139 --> 00:05:27,527
asked him to leave the room so she could
talk to it in private.

113
00:05:27,847 --> 00:05:29,916
We still call this the ELIZA effect:

114
00:05:29,916 --> 00:05:33,077
we read a mind into anything that talks
back fluently.

115
00:05:33,397 --> 00:05:34,510
Keep that in mind.

116
00:05:34,830 --> 00:05:35,790
It's still true.

117
00:05:36,950 --> 00:05:38,269
Then the money ran out.

118
00:05:38,589 --> 00:05:41,796
The promises had been too big, and the
computers too small.

119
00:05:42,116 --> 00:05:46,742
In the 1970s, and again in the late 1980s,
funding dried up.

120
00:05:47,062 --> 00:05:49,725
These stretches are called AI winters.

121
00:05:50,867 --> 00:05:52,197
1997.

122
00:05:52,517 --> 00:05:57,358
IBM's Deep Blue beats the world chess
champion, Garry Kasparov.

123
00:05:57,678 --> 00:05:59,262
But Deep Blue didn't learn chess.

124
00:05:59,582 --> 00:06:03,098
It searched around two hundred million
positions a second,

125
00:06:03,098 --> 00:06:06,493
using rules and evaluations its builders
wrote by hand.

126
00:06:07,658 --> 00:06:08,500
2012.

127
00:06:08,820 --> 00:06:10,552
Everything changes, quietly.

128
00:06:10,872 --> 00:06:16,788
A neural network called AlexNet enters a competition to recognise objects in
photos,

129
00:06:16,788 --> 00:06:18,689
and wins by a huge margin.

130
00:06:19,009 --> 00:06:23,648
It was trained on two graphics cards, the
same kind of chips built for video games.

131
00:06:23,968 --> 00:06:28,183
Graphics cards turned out to be very good
at the maths neural networks need.

132
00:06:28,503 --> 00:06:31,822
Since then, deep learning has taken over
almost the whole field.

133
00:06:32,992 --> 00:06:34,118
2017.

134
00:06:34,438 --> 00:06:38,992
Eight researchers at Google publish a
paper called "Attention Is All You Need".

135
00:06:39,312 --> 00:06:41,124
It introduces the transformer,

136
00:06:41,124 --> 00:06:44,417
a new design for neural networks that
handles language

137
00:06:44,417 --> 00:06:47,406
far better and can be trained at enormous
scale.

138
00:06:47,726 --> 00:06:50,716
The "T" in GPT stands for transformer.

139
00:06:51,867 --> 00:06:52,984
2022.

140
00:06:53,304 --> 00:06:57,737
A chatbot built on a transformer goes
public, and within weeks, it's everywhere.

141
00:06:58,867 --> 00:07:00,004
2023.

142
00:07:00,324 --> 00:07:03,588
Meta shares its language model, LLaMA,
with researchers.

143
00:07:03,908 --> 00:07:06,746
Within about a week, the weights leak to
the whole internet.

144
00:07:07,066 --> 00:07:09,359
People start running it on their own
laptops.

145
00:07:09,679 --> 00:07:13,347
Since then, a steady stream of open-weight
models has followed,

146
00:07:13,347 --> 00:07:16,258
from labs in the United States, France and
China.

147
00:07:16,578 --> 00:07:19,177
That stream is the reason this course can
exist.

148
00:07:20,325 --> 00:07:21,435
Notice the pattern.

149
00:07:21,755 --> 00:07:22,746
Big promises.

150
00:07:23,066 --> 00:07:23,978
Disappointment.

151
00:07:24,298 --> 00:07:25,399
A quiet breakthrough.

152
00:07:25,719 --> 00:07:26,976
Big promises again.

153
00:07:28,117 --> 00:07:31,199
What's different this time is three things
arriving together.

154
00:07:31,519 --> 00:07:33,219
Oceans of text from the internet.

155
00:07:33,539 --> 00:07:35,900
Cheap parallel computing from graphics
chips.

156
00:07:36,220 --> 00:07:38,440
And the transformer, to make use of both.

157
00:07:42,158 --> 00:07:45,000
So what can today's language models
actually do well?

158
00:07:45,460 --> 00:07:46,845
Honestly, quite a lot.

159
00:07:47,165 --> 00:07:49,339
They're good at drafting and rewriting
text.

160
00:07:49,659 --> 00:07:51,171
Summarising something long.

161
00:07:51,491 --> 00:07:52,355
Translating.

162
00:07:52,675 --> 00:07:56,708
Explaining an idea at your level, and then
again more simply if you ask.

163
00:07:57,028 --> 00:07:57,925
Brainstorming.

164
00:07:58,245 --> 00:07:59,338
Helping with code.

165
00:07:59,658 --> 00:08:02,118
Turning messy notes into something
organised.

166
00:08:03,283 --> 00:08:04,417
And what are they bad at?

167
00:08:04,877 --> 00:08:06,938
They can be wrong with complete
confidence.

168
00:08:07,258 --> 00:08:09,927
A language model produces what sounds
likely,

169
00:08:09,927 --> 00:08:12,894
and "sounds likely" is not the same as "is
true".

170
00:08:13,214 --> 00:08:17,143
When it invents facts, sources or quotes,
that's called a hallucination.

171
00:08:17,463 --> 00:08:19,947
It only knows what was in its training
data,

172
00:08:19,947 --> 00:08:23,448
up to a cut-off date, unless it's
connected to a search tool.

173
00:08:23,768 --> 00:08:27,869
It doesn't know your life, your files or
your situation unless you tell it.

174
00:08:29,033 --> 00:08:30,305
And remember ELIZA.

175
00:08:30,625 --> 00:08:32,746
Fluent isn't the same as understanding.

176
00:08:33,908 --> 00:08:35,215
Is it actually thinking?

177
00:08:35,675 --> 00:08:40,114
Serious researchers disagree, and we won't
pretend to settle it in lesson one.

178
00:08:40,434 --> 00:08:42,965
For this course, one practical rule is
enough.

179
00:08:44,117 --> 00:08:45,658
Treat it like a very fast,

180
00:08:45,658 --> 00:08:49,273
very well-read assistant who is sometimes
confidently wrong.

181
00:08:49,593 --> 00:08:50,264
Use it.

182
00:08:50,584 --> 00:08:51,414
Learn from it.

183
00:08:51,734 --> 00:08:52,809
Check what matters.

184
00:08:56,533 --> 00:08:57,397
Here's the map.

185
00:08:57,717 --> 00:08:59,171
Part one: understand.

186
00:08:59,491 --> 00:09:02,741
How a language model works, what a model
actually is,

187
00:09:02,741 --> 00:09:06,360
open versus closed, and whether your
computer can run one.

188
00:09:06,680 --> 00:09:08,296
No installing anything yet.

189
00:09:08,616 --> 00:09:09,819
Part two: run it.

190
00:09:10,139 --> 00:09:14,422
How to download safely, your first local
model without touching a terminal,

191
00:09:14,422 --> 00:09:18,019
then the terminal itself, choosing a
model, and using it well.

192
00:09:18,339 --> 00:09:19,884
Part three: make it yours.

193
00:09:20,204 --> 00:09:22,599
Chatting with your own documents
privately,

194
00:09:22,599 --> 00:09:25,995
voice and images, keeping it all healthy,
and passing it on.

195
00:09:27,158 --> 00:09:30,315
By the end, you'll have an AI that lives
on your machine.

196
00:09:30,635 --> 00:09:32,535
It answers with the cable unplugged.

197
00:09:32,855 --> 00:09:34,827
No one else sees your conversations.

198
00:09:35,147 --> 00:09:37,665
No one can raise the price or switch it
off.

199
00:09:38,825 --> 00:09:41,050
This course is one part of something
bigger.

200
00:09:41,370 --> 00:09:42,257
We're ainarchy,

201
00:09:42,257 --> 00:09:44,769
an open group of people who think everyone

202
00:09:44,769 --> 00:09:47,520
should be able to build with this
technology,

203
00:09:47,520 --> 00:09:48,762
freely and for free.

204
00:09:49,908 --> 00:09:54,734
Every lesson ends with a small task in our
Telegram group, in a room called BUILD.

205
00:09:55,054 --> 00:09:59,155
That's where the real learning happens:
people helping people, at every level.

206
00:10:00,325 --> 00:10:01,712
Your first task is easy.

207
00:10:02,032 --> 00:10:03,955
Go to BUILD and introduce yourself.

208
00:10:04,275 --> 00:10:06,773
Tell us what computer you have, if you
know,

209
00:10:06,773 --> 00:10:10,918
and one thing you'd like to do with an AI
that runs on your own machine.

210
00:10:11,238 --> 00:10:12,934
No personal details needed.

211
00:10:13,254 --> 00:10:13,961
That's it.

212
00:10:15,117 --> 00:10:17,828
Next time: how a language model actually
works,

213
00:10:17,828 --> 00:10:20,020
and why it sometimes makes things up.

214
00:10:20,340 --> 00:10:21,523
See you in lesson two.
